Your CI has duplicated Node setup, lint, type-check, test, and build steps. It also uses invalid GitHub Actions syntax: `if: exists('tsconfig.json')` is not supported, and `path: build || dist || .` is not a valid fallback expression. GitHub’s `setup-node` action already supports npm caching, while artifact paths should be supplied as valid files or directories. [1][2][3]

## Corrected workflow

```yaml
name: CI

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main
      - master
      - dev
      - pure-agent
      - root
      - develop
      - crystalcastle-ai

  pull_request:
    branches:
      - main
      - master
      - dev
      - pure-agent
      - root
      - develop
      - crystalcastle-ai

  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  build-and-test:
    name: Node.js ${{ matrix.node-version }}
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    strategy:
      fail-fast: true
      matrix:
        node-version: ["20", "22"]

    env:
      CI: true

    steps:
      - name: Checkout
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Node.js
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: ${{ matrix.node-version }}
          cache: npm

      - name: Validate package lock
        run: npm ci

      - name: Run lint
        run: npm run lint --if-present

      - name: Run type check
        run: npm run typecheck --if-present

      - name: Run tests
        run: npm test --if-present -- --runInBand

      - name: Build
        run: npm run build --if-present

      - name: Upload build artifact
        if: success()
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        with:
          name: build-node-${{ matrix.node-version }}
          path: |
            build
            dist
          if-no-files-found: ignore
```

## Important changes

- Removed the second `setup-node` step that always changed the matrix version to Node 22.
- Changed Node 18 to Node 20 and Node 22; use Node 18 only if your project explicitly requires it.
- Combined duplicate lint, type-check, test, and build steps.
- Replaced unsupported `exists('tsconfig.json')` with `npm run typecheck --if-present`.
- Replaced the invalid artifact expression with separate `build` and `dist` paths.
- Added `permissions: contents: read` for safer default permissions.

If your `package.json` has no `typecheck` script, the command will be skipped. The `--if-present` option is supported by npm and prevents a missing optional script from failing the job. [4]

## Optional package scripts

Your `package.json` should contain scripts similar to these:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "lint": "eslint .",
    "typecheck": "tsc --noEmit",
    "test": "jest",
    "build": "vite build"
  }
}
```

If the project uses a different test runner, replace `jest` with the correct command, such as `vitest run`.

